/P 325 0 R
/E (The Islamic State of Iraq and Syria \(ISIS\) grew out of Al-Qaeda in Iraq, capitalizing on the sectarian tensions following the 2003 US invasion of Iraq and the chaos of the 2011 Syrian civil war. In 2014, the group captured vast territories across both nations, declaring a global "caliphate" centered in Raqqa and ruling over millions with extreme brutality. Although a global coalition militarily dismantled its territorial caliphate by 2019, ISIS successfully transitioned back into a decentralized network of clandestine insurgent cells, maintaining an asymmetric low-level conflict. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
700 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (The insurgency operates primarily in the remote Badia Desert of Syria and the disputed territories of northern Iraq. ISIS avoids holding permanent ground, relying instead on hit-and-run tactics, roadside improvised explosive devices \(IEDs\), and targeted assassinations of local village leaders. This ongoing security threat occurs alongside the gradual drawdown of international coalition forces, shifting primary responsibility onto local militaries. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
701 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 325 0 R
/E (State security forces rely heavily on predictive policing and digital surveillance tools to combat the underground nature of ISIS cells. AI algorithms scan thousands of hours of commercial thermal drone footage to detect anomalies in desert terrain, mapping hidden underground bunkers or cave entrances. Security agencies also use automated NLP \(Natural Language Processing\) tools to track and disrupt encrypted communication channels used for propaganda distribution and financial transfers. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
